#. type: Title ##
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "1. From the model to a circuit {#1-from-the-model-to-a-circuit}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "The two-qubit gate {#the-two-qubit-gate}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Every bond $(i, j)$ of the lattice gets the same two-qubit gate, made of a "
"Heisenberg entangler followed by random fields:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"$$\n"
"\\Large\n"
"U_{ij} =  e^{iJ(X_iX_j + Y_iY_j + Z_iZ_j)}\\; e^{i(h_i Z_i + h_j Z_j)}\n"
"$$\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "with $h$ drawn uniformly from $[-\\pi/2, \\pi/2]$ for every gate.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"> **Question.** How would you decompose $U_{ij}$ into gates the hardware can"
" run?\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"<Accordion>\n"
"<AccordionItem title=\"Answer\">\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"**The fields** are easy. $e^{ihZ}$ is a single-qubit rotation $R_Z(-2h)$ on "
"each qubit.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"**The entangler** is the hard part. $XX$, $YY$ and $ZZ$ commute, so it "
"factors into three rotations, $R_{XX}$, $R_{YY}$ and $R_{ZZ}$. Each takes "
"two CNOTs, which makes six CNOTs per gate.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"There is a better way. Any two-qubit gate can be built from at most three "
"CNOTs and single-qubit rotations. The optimal circuit starts from the form "
"$e^{i(aXX + bYY + cZZ)}$, and this entangler already has that form with $a ="
" b = c = J$. So it maps straight onto three CNOTs, and the single-qubit "
"angles are linear in $J$. The experiment code builds the gate this way.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"On hardware, every two-qubit gate adds noise, so halving the CNOT count "
"matters.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"</AccordionItem>\n"
"</Accordion>\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"![Output of the previous code cell](/docs/images/tutorials/onset-of-"
"ergodicity/extracted-outputs/f04650ab-0.avif)\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"The brickwork and the Floquet cycle {#the-brickwork-and-the-floquet-cycle}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"The gates tile the square lattice in four *sublayers* of non-overlapping "
"bonds, a brickwork: horizontal bonds starting on even columns, then odd "
"columns, then vertical bonds starting on even rows, then odd rows. Qubit $y "
"\\cdot L_x + x$ sits at site $(x, y)$.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"The four sublayers make one *Floquet cycle*. The cycle repeats $n_F$ times with **the same fields**, so the dynamics are random in space and periodic in time. The circuit starts from the Néel state, and $n_F$ is half the side length of the lattice.\n"
"The next cell draws one Floquet cycle of the $8 \\times 8$ hardware lattice, sublayer by sublayer, with the fields of the hardware run, in the style of Figure 5 of the paper. Thick bonds are the gates of each sublayer. Each qubit a gate acts on is colored by the random field $h$ it then receives, and idle qubits are white. The arrows show the Néel initial state, with $\\uparrow$ for $|0\\rangle$. The paper's figure applies the vertical sublayers first; neither order is expected to change the physics.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"![Output of the previous code cell](/docs/images/tutorials/onset-of-"
"ergodicity/extracted-outputs/5f1a97ce-0.avif)\n"
msgstr ""

