#. type: Title ##
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "3. Noise, and how to undo it {#3-noise-and-how-to-undo-it}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "A noisy simulation {#a-noisy-simulation}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Now add noise: 1% two-qubit depolarizing noise on every CZ gate and a 3% readout error on every qubit, sampled with the Qiskit Aer sampler. With 144 CZ gates in the circuit, this is much less noise than the 8×8 hardware circuits see, but enough to see what it does. The simulation uses six couplings and 4,000 shots each, and takes about 1.5 minutes.\n"
"> **Question.** Before you run it: will noise make the system look more thermalized or less thermalized?\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"More. Noise scrambles the outcomes, which pushes every marginal distribution"
" toward uniform, so the raw IPR drops and the entropy rises. Without "
"mitigation, noise looks like thermalization that is not there, and the "
"crossovers come out too early.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"![Output of the previous code cell](/docs/images/tutorials/onset-of-"
"ergodicity/extracted-outputs/8680e8eb-0.avif)\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Brainstorm: how would you undo this? {#brainstorm-how-would-you-undo-this}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"<Accordion>\n"
"<AccordionItem title=\"Suggested approach\">\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"At small $J$, the state is barely entangled, so the ideal answer there is "
"cheap to compute classically, even for 64 qubits. That suggests measuring "
"the effect of the noise at a small calibration coupling $J_0$ and correcting"
" every other coupling with it. There are two natural ways to do that, called"
" low-entanglement calibration (LEC):\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"**LEC on the IPR.** Rescale the excess of the IPR over its value for a "
"uniform distribution, $2^{-n_A}$, by one factor per marginal:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"$$\n"
"\\Large\n"
"\\mathrm{IPR}^{\\mathrm{LEC}}_A(J) = 2^{-n_A} + R_0\\bigl(\\mathrm{IPR}^{\\mathrm{raw}}_A(J) - 2^{-n_A}\\bigr), \\qquad R_0 = \\frac{\\mathrm{IPR}^{\\mathrm{ideal}}_A(J_0) - 2^{-n_A}}{\\mathrm{IPR}^{\\mathrm{raw}}_A(J_0) - 2^{-n_A}}\n"
"$$\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"**LEC on the $\\langle Z \\rangle$ expectation values.** Rescale every "
"parity of the marginal by its own factor, and rebuild the IPR with the "
"Parseval formula:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"$$\n"
"\\Large\n"
"r_S = \\frac{\\langle Z_S \\rangle^{\\mathrm{ideal}}(J_0)}{\\langle Z_S \\rangle^{\\mathrm{raw}}(J_0)}, \\qquad\n"
"\\mathrm{IPR}^{\\mathrm{LEC}}_A(J) = 2^{-n_A}\\Bigl(1 + \\sum_{S} r_S^2 \\, \\langle Z_S \\rangle^{\\mathrm{raw}}(J)^2\\Bigr)\n"
"$$\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"In the code, each squared rescaled parity is debiased by subtracting its "
"variance, as in section 2.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Both are the same if every $r_S^2$ equals $R_0$.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"</AccordionItem>\n"
"</Accordion>\n"
"> **Question.** What are the pros and cons of these methods?\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "**Pros**\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '- '
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"**Cheap:** no extra data is needed from the device, only a classical "
"simulation at $J_0$.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '- '
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"**Uses the structure of the circuit:** the circuit depth and the CZ layers "
"do not change with $J$, so the noise at $J_0$ is the same noise as at every "
"other $J$.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "**Cons**\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '- '
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"**Assumes the effect of the noise on the observables does not depend on "
"$J$:** the noise itself is the same, but how it damps the IPR or each "
"$\\langle Z_S \\rangle$ can change as $J$ grows.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Both methods on the noisy simulation {#both-methods-on-the-noisy-simulation}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"![Output of the previous code cell](/docs/images/tutorials/onset-of-"
"ergodicity/extracted-outputs/212cb53d-0.avif)\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Both methods remove most of the damage: the raw estimates in the previous "
"plot are far too random, and the mitigated ones follow the exact curves. The"
" differences show up for the larger marginals.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"For the small marginals both methods work. For the $3 \\times 3$ marginal, "
"LEC on $\\langle Z \\rangle$ is clearly closer.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"**Why LEC on the IPR gives too little entropy.** Too little entropy means "
"the IPR is too big: LEC on the IPR over-inflates it. It assumes that noise "
"damps the contribution of every $\\langle Z_S \\rangle$ to the IPR by the "
"same factor. At $J_0$, every $\\langle Z_S \\rangle$ is close to $\\pm 1$, "
"so all strings contribute equally, and most strings have medium weight: of "
"the 511 strings of a $3 \\times 3$ marginal, 252 have weight 4 or 5, and "
"only nine have weight 1. Noise damps a string more the higher its weight "
"(readout error alone damps a weight-$w$ string by $0.94^w$), so the single "
"factor $R_0$ is set mainly by the damping of medium-weight strings. As $J$ "
"grows, the higher-weight $\\langle Z_S \\rangle$ decay fastest, and the IPR "
"is carried more and more by low-weight strings, which noise damps less. "
"Boosting them with a factor learned mainly from more heavily damped strings "
"over-inflates the IPR. The effect grows with the size of the marginal, since"
" a larger marginal has a wider range of weights. It is largest at "
"intermediate $J$, after the mix of strings has shifted but while the excess "
"of the IPR over its uniform value still matters.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"**Why LEC on $\\langle Z \\rangle$ errs the other way.** It rescales each "
"string by its own factor, so the shift between weights does not affect it. "
"It only assumes that each string keeps its $J_0$ damping. As $J$ grows, each"
" $Z_S$ spreads into higher-weight operators, which pick up more noise than "
"at $J_0$, so the factors slightly under-correct and the entropy comes out "
"slightly too high.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"So the prediction to test on hardware is: **the larger the marginal, the "
"more LEC on $\\langle Z \\rangle$ should win**. On hardware, the noise is "
"stronger and not of a known form.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "4. Run at scale {#4-run-at-scale}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"The hardware experiment runs an $8 \\times 8$ lattice with four cycles: 64 "
"qubits and 1,344 CZ gates in 48 layers, at nine couplings. Five things "
"change compared with the simulation:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '1. '
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"The noise has to be **learned**, which only works if the CZ layers are "
"fixed.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '2. '
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"**Twirling** turns the noise of each layer into a Pauli channel, which can "
"be learned and amplified.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '3. '
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "**Dynamical decoupling (DD)** protects idle qubits.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '4. '
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "**Noise amplification** for PEA runs in the same job as the main data.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '5. '
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"**MPS simulations** at small $J$ provide the classical references for LEC-"
"based error mitigation.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"The cells that need a backend only run when `RUN_ON_HARDWARE` is `True`. MPS"
" simulations can be rerun with `COMPUTE_MPS_REFERENCES` set to `True`. The "
"other cells run here.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Fixed CZ layers {#fixed-cz-layers}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Boxes for noise learning {#boxes-for-noise-learning}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"The Samplomatic boxing pass manager then groups each circuit into *boxes*: "
"one per CZ layer and one for the measurements. Each box carries a reference "
"to the noise model it needs, so you can check that all couplings share the "
"same boxes. The next cell does this for the $8 \\times 8$ circuits of the "
"hardware run, compiled to CZ gates without a backend, and prints the boxes "
"that noise learning will characterize.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Twirl the circuits {#twirl-the-circuits}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"In every randomization, Samplomatic puts random Pauli gates before each box "
"and the compensating gates after it, merged into the neighboring single-"
"qubit gates. Averaged over randomizations, the noise of each layer becomes a"
" Pauli channel, which `NoiseLearnerV3` can learn as a sparse Pauli-Lindblad "
"model and the PEA step can amplify. Measurement twirling randomly flips bits"
" before readout. The flips are undone in software, which makes the readout "
"noise symmetric and is the basis of the readout correction (TREX).\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "The hardware workflow {#the-hardware-workflow}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Choose the qubits, compile the circuits, then learn the noise and run the "
"sweep. These cells are switched off here.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"**Choose the qubits.** Nighthawk's square grid holds the $8 \\times 8$ "
"lattice without SWAP gates. The cell scores every $8 \\times 8$ rectangle of"
" the grid by its reported CZ and readout errors and takes the best.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"**Compile.** The same steps as above on the chosen qubits, followed by "
"`build`, which turns each boxed circuit into a template circuit and a "
"*samplex*, the recipe for drawing the twirling gates and the injected noise."
" DD is added to the templates.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"**Learn the noise and run the sweep.** One `NoiseLearnerV3` job learns all "
"five boxes: a Pauli-Lindblad model for each of the four CZ layers, and the "
"readout noise of the measurement box. Then one `QuantumProgram` holds all "
"nine couplings. Each item gets its template, its samplex, the learned noise "
"models, and three noise scales. A scale of 0 is the unamplified data for "
"both LEC methods. Scales of 0.5 and 1 add 50% and 100% of the learned noise "
"for PEA, which extrapolates back to zero noise. This uses about 40 seconds "
"of QPU time.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "5. The hardware data {#5-the-hardware-data}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"This section explores how the previous methods perform on real hardware data"
" from a Nighthawk processor.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Load the run {#load-the-run}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"With `RUN_ON_HARDWARE` set to `True`, the next cell converts the results of "
"the two jobs from section 4 and saves them to `data/hardware_run_new.npz`, "
"in the same format as the saved run, so the new run can be analyzed again "
"later. Otherwise it loads the saved run from `data/hardware_run.npz`. The "
"bits are packed along the qubit axis to save space.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"If the kernel restarts while the jobs run, do not submit them again. Rerun "
"the cells before the last call in section 4, check that the same qubits are "
"chosen, and set `learner_job` and `executor_job` to `service.job(job_id)` "
"with the job IDs that were printed out in section 4.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Classical references {#classical-references}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Both LEC methods need the ideal marginal distributions at $J_0 = 0.005\\pi$,"
" and testing them needs ideal values at the held-out couplings $J = "
"0.01\\pi$ and $0.02\\pi$. These come from matrix product state (MPS) "
"simulations, saved for every placement of each marginal shape on the "
"lattice, as in the paper, and for every non-overlapping $2 \\times 2$ block "
"at $J_0$. At $J_0$ they converge at bond dimension $\\chi = 64$. At $J = "
"0.01\\pi$ and $0.02\\pi$ they use $\\chi = 128$, and at $0.02\\pi$ they "
"still change by up to about 13% from $\\chi = 64$ for $3 \\times 3$ "
"marginals, so treat them as approximate there. The next cell computes them "
"if `COMPUTE_MPS_REFERENCES` is `True`, which takes about 18 minutes, and "
"otherwise loads the saved results, keyed by coupling index, bond dimension, "
"and marginal.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Data quality: faulty couplers / qubits {#data-quality-faulty-couplers--"
"qubits}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"First, look at the lowest coupling, $J_0$, where the expectation values "
"should be close to the Néel checkerboard.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"![Output of the previous code cell](/docs/images/tutorials/onset-of-"
"ergodicity/extracted-outputs/48d2dbbb-0.avif)\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "> **Question.** Looking at the hardware map, what stands out?\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"The pale sites, which have lost almost all of their signal although the "
"state at $J_0$ is close to the Néel state. They matter because LEC divides "
"by whatever signal survives at $J_0$: a qubit with almost no signal gives a "
"huge, noisy correction factor, and a few such qubits can dominate every "
"marginal that contains them.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"A rule that uses only the data: A qubit is faulty if its single-qubit IPR at"
" $J_0$ is within $0.25^2/2 \\approx 0.03$ of $1/2$, the value for a fully "
"mixed qubit. Since the IPR of one qubit is $(1 + \\langle Z \\rangle^2)/2$, "
"this flags qubits with $|\\langle Z \\rangle| < 0.25$. Marginals that "
"contain a flagged site are left out below.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Select the marginals to average {#select-the-marginals-to-average}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"The next cell shows which placements of each shape enter the averages below:"
" every placement in the bulk of the lattice without a flagged site (red "
"crosses). Placements that touch the edge (shaded) are left out. Edge qubits "
"have fewer bonds, so they scramble more slowly. Averaged on their own, they "
"cross over a full step of $J$ later than bulk placements, for every shape "
"and method. Each averaged placement has its own box, colored by placement.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"![Output of the previous code cell](/docs/images/tutorials/onset-of-"
"ergodicity/extracted-outputs/d31b217b-0.avif)\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "PEA {#pea}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"The third method needs no classical reference. For each parity, take the "
"values at noise factors 1, 1.5, and 2, divide out the learned readout "
"damping (TREX), fit an exponential in the noise factor, and extrapolate to "
"zero noise. Parities that change sign or are nearly zero get a straight-line"
" fit. Subtracting the variance needs a reliable estimate of it, which is "
"easy for raw and rescaled parities but not for the output of a nonlinear fit"
" with clipping and fallbacks. PEA therefore uses the other standard way to "
"remove the bias of a square. Extrapolate each half of the randomizations "
"separately and multiply the two independent estimates.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"All four estimates, and the crossovers {#all-four-estimates-and-the-"
"crossovers}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"The next function computes the raw, LEC-on-IPR, LEC-on-$\\langle Z "
"\\rangle$, and PEA estimates of the IPR of one marginal position at every "
"coupling. It also estimates the shot noise of each, by bootstrapping the "
"randomizations. The cell runs it for every placement that enters the "
"averages, which takes about a minute and a half.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"![Output of the previous code cell](/docs/images/tutorials/onset-of-"
"ergodicity/extracted-outputs/6303dec9-0.avif)\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Wrap-up {#wrap-up}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "What it took to run an experiment of this size:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '- '
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"**Circuits whose structure does not depend on $J$**, compiled one sublayer "
"at a time, so one noise model serves the whole sweep.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '- '
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"**Estimators that work from bitstrings**: pair counting for the IPR of a "
"marginal, and the Parseval form, with the variance subtracted from each "
"squared parity, when the inputs are mitigated expectation values.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '- '
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"**A cheap classical anchor**: the ideal state at small $J$ calibrates the "
"noise, and held-out couplings test the calibration. Calibrating each "
"$\\langle Z_S \\rangle$ separately, rather than the IPR as a whole, is what "
"makes the large marginals agree.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '- '
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"**IBM tools for the heavy lifting**: Samplomatic boxes and twirling, "
"`NoiseLearnerV3`, dynamical decoupling, and one `Executor` job with noise "
"amplification for PEA.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '- '
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "**Data-quality checks** that use only the data.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"**Next steps.** To go further with mitigation, see the [probabilistic error "
"amplification](/tutorials/probabilistic-error-amplification) and [PEC with "
"shaded lightcones](/tutorials/pec-with-shaded-lightcones) tutorials.\n"
msgstr ""

